;; THE ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA of the scored table engine (TR-design), PRE-REGISTERED on 2026-10-29 at main a39535b1, before any implementation.
;; `bb tables-ab compare --a A --b B` (and `bb tables-ab ab`) applies them to two runs of ONE build: A = `--tables=legacy`, B = `--tables=scored`.
;; B is accepted only when EVERY check holds. Changing a number here after the implementation starts is a new decision (DEVLOG) and voids
;; the comparisons made under the old one. The justification of each number is in .claude/coord/tr-design-report.md section 3.
{:populations ["synth" "icdar2013" "fintabnet" "ctdar" "ledgers" "named" "heldout"]
 ;; the aggregate: the mean of these populations' headlines (held-out is judged on its own, controls by words and cost)
 :aggregate ["synth" "icdar2013" "fintabnet" "ctdar" "ledgers" "named"]
 ;; 1. better or equal on every population's headline (replays are deterministic: equal means equal; scores are rounded to 1e-6)
 :tolerance 0.0
 ;;    and no single component metric of any population worse by more than this (a headline may not hide a broken component)
 :metric-tolerance 0.01
 ;; 2. strictly better on the aggregate by this absolute margin, and on the held-out sample by this margin with a paired
 ;;    bootstrap 95% lower bound above zero
 :aggregate-margin 0.01
 :heldout-margin 0.005
 ;; 3. no protected triple lost: structural ownership changed = 0 (only test/tables-ab/repairs.json may excuse a change)
 ;; 4. zero words lost and zero gained on the 54 controls
 ;; 5. cost: total CPU of B over A, replayed populations (the engine without OCR) and live controls; peak resident memory
 :replay-cost ["synth" "icdar2013" "fintabnet" "ctdar" "ledgers" "named" "heldout"]
 :cpu-replay 1.5
 :cpu-live 1.10
 :rss 1.25}
